; ============================================================
; TITLE:    Fibonacci Clocks
; CATEGORY: Number Theory
; LEVEL:    Advanced
; TAGS:     fibonacci, modular arithmetic, pisano period, string art
; SUMMARY:  Runs the Fibonacci sequence on clocks of different sizes, where it always repeats, and draws its path as colorful string art.
; ============================================================
;
; WHAT IT DOES
;   The Fibonacci numbers 0 1 1 2 3 5 8 13 21 ... grow forever. But keep
;   only the remainder after dividing by some number m (that is, run
;   them on an m-hour clock) and they must eventually repeat. There are
;   only m * m possible pairs of neighbors, and each pair decides the
;   next one. On a 10-hour clock (the last digit of each Fibonacci
;   number) the pattern repeats every 60 steps. These repeat lengths
;   are called Pisano periods. Each circle has m points; the program
;   joins every remainder to the next one with a line, so the path of
;   one full period becomes a piece of string art.
;
; HOW TO USE
;   Press Run. Change the :CLOCKS list to see other moduli. The Pisano
;   periods for 2 to 30 are printed in the command center.
;
; CONCEPTS YOU WILL LEARN
;   * MODULO keeps numbers small, so the sequence never overflows
;   * detecting a cycle: stop when the starting pair 0, 1 comes back
;   * points on a circle with SIN and COS
;   * shading colors along a path to show its direction in time
;
; THINGS TO TRY
;   * Look at the printed periods for 5, 25 and 125. What is the rule?
;   * Instead of joining F(k) to F(k+1), join F(k) to F(k+2) and compare.
; ============================================================

; Length of the cycle of Fibonacci numbers modulo :m. We walk pairs
; (a, b) -> (b, a + b) until the very first pair (0, 1) comes back.
to pisano :m
  localmake "a 0
  localmake "b 1
  localmake "steps 0
  do.until [
    localmake "c modulo :a + :b :m
    make "a :b
    make "b :c
    make "steps :steps + 1
  ] [and :a = 0 :b = 1]
  output :steps
end

; Point number :k of :m around a circle centered at (cx, cy).
to rim :k :m :cx :cy :r
  localmake "angle :k * 360 / :m
  output (list :cx + :r * sin :angle  :cy + :r * cos :angle)
end

; Shade from blue at the start of the period to orange at the end.
to shade :t
  output (list 40 + 210 * :t  90 + 60 * :t  220 - 190 * :t)
end

to fib.clock :m :cx :cy
  localmake "r 100
  localmake "period pisano :m
  ; the rim and its numbered points
  setpencolor [200 200 210] setpensize 1
  penup setxy :cx :cy setheading 0 pendown arc 360 :r penup
  setlabelheight 11
  for [k 0 :m - 1] [
    setpencolor [90 90 110] setpensize 5 dot rim :k :m :cx :cy :r
    penup setpos rim :k :m :cx :cy :r + 12
    setxy xcor - 3 * count :k ycor - 4
    label :k
  ]
  ; the string art: one chord per step of the period
  localmake "a 0
  localmake "b 1
  setpensize 1.5
  for [step 1 :period] [
    setpencolor shade :step / :period
    penup setpos rim :a :m :cx :cy :r
    pendown setpos rim :b :m :cx :cy :r
    localmake "c modulo :a + :b :m
    make "a :b
    make "b :c
  ]
  penup setxy :cx - 50 :cy - :r - 34 setpencolor [40 40 40] setlabelheight 14
  label (sentence "mod :m [ period] :period)
end

make "clocks [7 9 10 11 13 16]

clearscreen hideturtle
setbackground [252 252 250]
make "i 0
foreach :clocks [
  fib.clock ? -255 + 255 * remainder :i 3  135 - 285 * int (:i / 3)
  make "i :i + 1
]

print [Pisano periods, modulus then period:]
for [m 2 30] [type (word :m ": pisano :m "|   |)]
print []
